        {
          "context_before": [
            "",
            "Nobody compiles a slide. Imports, types the audience already knows, error handling and logging go unless they are the point, and the notes say what was cut when it matters. Rename to the words the talk uses. When code on a slide would not compile as shown, the notes say so."
          ],
          "removed": [],
          "added": [
            "",
            "## Types from TypeScript",
            "",
            "`twoslash` after the language (`ts`, `tsx`, `js`, `jsx`) runs TypeScript on the block when the deck builds. Use it when the types are the point of the slide:",
            "",
            "- `// ^?` under a line pins the type of what the caret points at, in a box under the line. It shows in print, so it can carry the slide's evidence.",
            "- `// @errors: 2322` shows an error the code is meant to have, underlined, with the message under the line. Any error the block does not declare stops the build, so a block that builds compiles.",
            "- `// ---cut---` hides the setup above it, and `// @noErrors` accepts every error, which completions (`// ^|`) need.",
            "- Hovering a name shows its type, for the audience of the published deck; a talk cannot rely on it.",
            "",
            "Every pinned type and error adds a line or more of height, so count them against the budget above. A block is a module at the root of the deck: it can import the deck's dependencies and `./snippets/…`. Twoslash does not work with `diff` or magic moves.",
            "",
            "`````mdx",
            "## A const object keeps its literal types",
            "",
            "```ts twoslash",
            "const config = { mode: \"fast\", retries: 3 } as const",
            "//    ^?",
            "```",
            "`````"
          ],
          "context_after": []
        }
      ]
